// TPR-07-019 topology pin: two unrelated take-projects whose results
// converge at a phi-style merge.
//
// The TPR-07-017 fix used a single union-find that unconditionally
// unioned every `Jump arg → block param` edge. At a multi-predecessor
// merge block, that turned phi-style alternatives into false alias
// equivalences: if predecessor 1 jumps with `%it_a` and predecessor 2
// jumps with `%it_b` into the same block param `%result`, the union-
// find collapsed `%it_a`, `%it_b`, and `%result` into one class even
// though `%result` is a CFG choice, not shared storage.
//
// The TPR-07-019 fix in `take_project::union_alias_edges` only unions
// Jump-arg → block-param edges when the target block has exactly one
// predecessor (a degenerate phi semantically equivalent to `let
// param = arg`). Multi-predecessor block params are phi merges and
// are NOT unified via this edge.
//
// This topology pin exercises the shape: two `MaybeIter` sources `a`
// and `b` are matched in separate `if` branches, each branch's match
// arm produces an `int` result, and those results meet at the `if`'s
// phi-style merge. With the old code, the IR shape would unify
// unrelated alias chains; with the fix, each take-project source
// stays in its own class.
//
// Runtime: pick = false → match `b` → it.count() = 3. `a` is on the
// bypass path and must drop via its own per-class scope-exit drop.
// Returns `result - 3 = 0` regardless of which path runs.

type MaybeIter = Empty | Holds(it: Iterator<int>);

@main () -> int = {
    let a: MaybeIter = Holds(it: [1, 2, 3].iter());
    let b: MaybeIter = Holds(it: [4, 5, 6].iter());
    let pick = false;
    let result = if pick then
        match a {
            Empty -> 0,
            Holds(it) -> it.count()
        }
    else
        match b {
            Empty -> 0,
            Holds(it) -> it.count()
        };
    result - 3
}
